---
import InsightArticle from '../../layouts/InsightArticle.astro';
import WorkedTable from '../../components/insights/WorkedTable.astro';
import PractitionerNote from '../../components/insights/PractitionerNote.astro';
import { practitionerB1 } from '../../data/practitioner-b1';
import examples from '../../data/insights/2026-11-14-practitioner.json';
const e = practitionerB1[1];
---
<InsightArticle title={e.title} description={e.description} date="2026-11-14" type="Analysis" readTime="5 min" standfirst="In our worked example, a candidate with the smaller API bill costs more per usable result. Count the answers you can accept before deciding which model is cheap.">
  <p>Candidate A costs $8 for a test run. Candidate B costs $12. On spend alone, A wins. Now suppose you accept 800 of A's results and 960 of B's. A costs $10 per thousand accepted results; B costs $12.50. A still wins, but the advantage is smaller than its bill suggested. A third candidate spends the same $8 as A and passes only 500 cases. Its cost per thousand accepted results is $16.</p>
  <p>Those are invented numbers. We have not run these candidates or assigned the figures to real models. The point is the unit: spend divided by useful results. It is a measure you can add to an AI tracker without importing somebody else's benchmark or pretending your own work resembles theirs.</p>
  <WorkedTable headers={['Fictional candidate', 'Attempts', 'API spend', 'Accepted', 'Cost per 1,000 accepted']} rows={examples.acceptance} caption="Illustrative counts and spend. Cost per 1,000 accepted results = total spend ÷ accepted count × 1,000. No retries or human costs are added separately." />
  <h2>Decide what an accepted result is</h2>
  <p>For a document extractor, your rule might require the correct value and a passage that supports it. For a label, it might require an exact match to a reviewed answer. For a draft, it might mean the editor can publish it after a defined amount of checking. Write that rule before seeing the results.</p>
  <p>A parsing success is a different measure. Anthropic's <a href={e.sources[0].url}>structured-output documentation</a>, read on 14 November 2026, describes schema-constrained responses and separate handling for refusals and token-limit stops. A format check does not prove that the extracted amount matches the document. Keep format validity and factual acceptance apart in your results.</p>
  <p>For judgement-heavy work, preserve the review note. “Good enough” becomes hard to compare when one reviewer means readable and another means ready to send. If the definition changes halfway through the test, mark the change and review the earlier examples again.</p>
  <h2>Charge failed attempts to the job</h2>
  <p>Use the total API spend for the test, including attempts that produced nothing usable. Dividing only the spend on successful requests by the successful count quietly removes the price of failure. If the application retries, include that spend too.</p>
  <p>Choose the denominator carefully. For a workflow that tries one document three times, those are three attempts at one job. If the final result is accepted, count one accepted job. For a comparison with no retries, one attempt per case is simpler. Do not mix the two definitions and call the result an improvement.</p>
  <p>A test with zero accepted results has no finite cost per accepted result. Show that plainly. Turning it into $0 because a division failed would make the worst candidate look free.</p>
  <h2>Keep the other limits visible</h2>
  <p>A lower cost per accepted result can still be unsuitable. You may need a response within a particular time, or have one kind of error you will not accept. Put those conditions beside the cost. Do not trade them away through an average.</p>
  <p>Human review can change the result again. If you measure it, report the time separately before putting a money value on it. State the hourly rate you assume and whether you timed the checking or merely estimated it. An API bill is observable; a guessed labour saving is an assumption.</p>
  <p>Also report the case count. Passing a tiny hand-picked set does not establish a stable rate for the next month's work. Keep a held-back set that you did not use to improve the prompt, and retain the failures. If the application meets a new kind of document, add a category rather than silently replacing difficult old examples.</p>
  <h2>The tracker row worth keeping</h2>
  <p>Store total spend, job count, attempt count, accepted count and the acceptance rule. Add model and route identifiers, prompt version and test date. Derive the cost from those fields, leaving the original counts visible. That makes a later comparison possible even if your preferred display unit changes.</p>
  <p>The next time a model cuts its token price, rerun the jobs. The useful question is how much it now costs to produce an answer you can use.</p>
  <PractitionerNote evidence={e} />
</InsightArticle>
